you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news coming up on today's  programme  boris johnson insists that cop 26 can be  the beginning of the end of climate  change but it seems even conservative  voters don't trust him on the issue  ending climate change will mean 1.5  needs to stay alive we'll look at the  six actions published today aimed at  keeping global warming below one and a  half degrees  and lab grown meat could apparently add  2 billion pounds to the uk economy so  should it be given the go-ahead  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and challenge those coming up with the  solutions  there's now a month to go until the cop  26 climate summit gets underway in  glasgow and today it's president alex  sharma revealed that more than 100 world  leaders have confirmed that they will  attend in person although china which  will still send a delegation is not one  of them well despite that apparent  setback boris johnson who today  addressed the youth cop event in milan  by video link insisted that the summit  could be the beginning of the end of  climate change so long as those world  leaders address the four issues that are  fast becoming a mantra for him  there are four things that we want to  come out of  of cop 26 for for big changes in our  approach and and it's very easy to  remember it's coal cars cash and trees  coal cars cash and trees we want we want  to move away as i've described want to  move away from using coal as uh the way  we generate uh electricity we want to  move towards renewable all the countries  in the world to to move off coal  but there's bad news for mr johnson on  the environment front this evening  courtesy of a new poll shared  exclusively with sky news our climate  change correspondent hannah thomas peter  is in milan with the details so hannah  what have you learned then  well the pollen's been done by the  conservative environment network so you  you'd expect them to ask questions that  might be favorable for the prime  minister but actually the polling has  revealed  that just 25  of adults polled think that a  conservative government led by boris  johnson is the best choice to handle  climate change and environment issues  but 32 percent of those same adults  thought that kia stammer would be a  better option and actually only 50  percent of the conservative party's own  voters thought that boris johnson would  be the best choice on environment issues  and a significant proportion of those  surveyed thought that the government is  undercooking its response  to climate change now  those headlines will not be comfortable  reading for boris johnson but there is  some good news hidden in there which is  that the government's net zero approach  to get to net zero carbon emissions by  2050 enjoys very broad cross-party  support and actually voters want the  government to do more and think that it  should be a top priority when it comes  to spending that will give boris johnson  a mandate when he reads this poll uh to  to go further to spend more and he's  certainly going to have to do that the  government has not yet published its net  zero strategy or its homes and building  strategy which is how to decarbonize the  heating in millions of british homes  that's a domestic context but as you say  a huge amount of international diplomacy  going on now a big last push  into the cop 26 meeting in glasgow and  lots of problems left to resolve will  there be enough cash on the table for  developing nations will there be more  enhanced commitments to reducing carbon  emissions and will there be an agreement  before cop 26 from big coal consumers  and producers to finally phase out coal  for good  hannah thanks very much indeed  well if cop26 really is going to be the  beginning of the end of climate change  as boris johnson hopes whatever's  decided in glasgow needs to curtail the  rise in global warming well today the  energy transitions commission published  the six actions that it hopes can keep  1.5 alive describing one of them is  particularly crucial that's cutting  methane emissions by 40  by 2030  and so the first action is significant  and rapid reduction in methane emissions  now the gas mainly released through  agriculture especially cows and the  burning of fossil fuels is estimated to  be responsible for up to 40 percent of  global warming so far  well next is halting deforestation and  beginning reforestation and that could  reduce greenhouse gas emissions by six  and a half gigatons by 2030 and that's  about a fifth of the planet's entire  emissions  the third action is decarbonizing the  power sector now an immediate ban on new  coal-fired power plants and the phase  out of existing plants could reduce  emissions by a further three and a half  gigatons  now accelerating the shift to electric  vehicles could reduce emissions by an  additional 2.3 gigatons while reducing  emissions from buildings industry and  heavy transport would save another  gigaton  and finally there's a call for energy  and resource efficiency to be  reinvigorated an international agreement  to improve the energy efficiency of new  buildings could deliver an additional  1.4 gigaton reduction of co2 by 2030.  well lord turner is the chair of the  energy transitions commission and he  joins me now lord turner is keeping 1.5  alive then doable and affordable  it's undoubtedly technically doable and  many of the actions that we need to take  which you've just described have either  negative costs or very low costs i would  say four out of those six categories  really don't cost the global economy  anything there are things where the  technologies are driving us forward  towards progress and we just have to  seize that opportunity two of them  ending deforestation and phasing out  existing coal as well as ending new coal  that does involve a cost and we've got  to work out who's going to pay for it  and whether the developed world is going  to help the developing world with that  which is why that fourth of what boris  johnson talked about cash is an  important thing as well so i think on  those two we got to have a debate about  who's going to bear some actual cost to  get there on the other four i think we  should be confident that the  technologies will get us there at a  relatively low cost as well as as long  as we force the pace of change well yes  and we have known for some time haven't  we that the actions you've identified  can reduce emissions so how confident  are you that your report will make any  difference it'll make governments and  industry change their habits  well look you're absolutely right that  broadly speaking the actions we need to  take to reduce emissions are  there at one level obvious they are  known already i think one of the things  that we're doing is drawing attention to  methane there hasn't been enough  attention on on methane as well as co2  but in relation to the others what we've  done is clearly work out their relative  importance work out how much difference  one could actually make as early as 2030  and try to identify the specific levers  that could be pulled at cop26  by  leading groups of countries and  companies without having to get  agreement with all 196 countries and  those sort of leading coalition  opportunities are ones which we should  try to seize okay lord turner thanks  very much indeed for your time this  evening  thank you  well let me take you over now to our  climate data dashboard and show you just  how hard a task is going to be to keep  global warming below one and a half  degrees now temperatures have already  increased as you can see here by more  than 1.23 degrees celsius since  pre-industrial levels and the current  estimate is that will hit that crucial  level in just over 11 years  you

